    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d564">

                <pb facs="0462" n="450" xml:id="pg450"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/4517: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé at Tangier</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Childs</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tangier</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-28T20:00:00+01:00">January 28, 1942—8
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>33. Legation’s despatches No. 475<anchor n="17" xml:id="d564-f1"/> and 432 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">532</hi>].<anchor n="18" xml:id="d564-f2"/> My British
                    colleague recommended to his Government that the approach mentioned in last
                    paragraph, page 2, despatch No. 475<anchor n="19" xml:id="d564-f3"/> be made. He
                    has now hinted that the situation would not appear to be propitious to attempt
                    any but the most restrained approach at the present time without raising any
                    present hope of aid or inviting a request for such.</p>
                <p>The situation which has developed within the last few days in Libya<anchor n="20"
                        xml:id="d564-f4"/> appears to us all, including in particular, Colonel
                        Eddy,<anchor n="20a" xml:id="d564-f5"/> to make this an inappropriate time
                    for us to raise the subject and we are accordingly taking no action at this
                    time.</p>
                <p>Full air mail report despatch no. 585<anchor n="18" xml:id="d564-f6"/> leaving
                    tomorrow.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Childs</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d564-f1" n="17" xml:id="d564-f1-content">
                <p>Dated December 9, 1941, <ref target="#pg439">p. 439</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f2" n="18" xml:id="d564-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f3" n="19" xml:id="d564-f3-content">
                <p>i. e., first paragraph on <ref target="#pg440">p. 440</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f4" n="20" xml:id="d564-f4-content">
                <p>On January 21 German forces under General Rommel in Libya began the drive to the
                    east.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f5" n="20a" xml:id="d564-f5-content">
                <p>Lt. Col. William A. Eddy, Naval Attaché at Tangier.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d564-f6" n="18" xml:id="d564-f6-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
